            </style><div class="popup" style="position:fixed;width:100vw;height:100vh;background:rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);left:0;top:0;z-index:100;backdrop-filter:blur(5px) brightness(0.5);justify-content:center;align-items:center;display:none"><svg style="position:absolute;top:3vh;right:3vw;z-index:2;background:rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.2);width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:4px;cursor:pointer" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M13.5909 12L18.0441 7.54687C18.2554 7.3359 18.3743 7.04962 18.3745 6.75099C18.3748 6.45237 18.2564 6.16587 18.0455 5.95453C17.8345 5.74319 17.5482 5.62431 17.2496 5.62404C16.951 5.62378 16.6645 5.74215 16.4531 5.95312L12 10.4062L7.54687 5.95312C7.33553 5.74178 7.04888 5.62305 6.75 5.62305C6.45111 5.62305 6.16447 5.74178 5.95312 5.95312C5.74178 6.16447 5.62305 6.45111 5.62305 6.75C5.62305 7.04888 5.74178 7.33553 5.95312 7.54687L10.4062 12L5.95312 16.4531C5.74178 16.6645 5.62305 16.9511 5.62305 17.25C5.62305 17.5489 5.74178 17.8355 5.95312 18.0469C6.16447 18.2582 6.45111 18.3769 6.75 18.3769C7.04888 18.3769 7.33553 18.2582 7.54687 18.0469L12 13.5937L16.4531 18.0469C16.6645 18.2582 16.9511 18.3769 17.25 18.3769C17.5489 18.3769 17.8355 18.2582 18.0469 18.0469C18.2582 17.8355 18.3769 17.5489 18.3769 17.25C18.3769 16.9511 18.2582 16.6645 18.0469 16.4531L13.5909 12Z" fill="white"/></svg><img src alt="Popup Image" style="max-width:90vw;max-height:90vh;object-fit:contain;cursor:zoom-out"></div><div class="blog-container" style="display:flex;justify-content:flex-start;align-items:flex-start;overflow:visible;position:relative;flex-direction:column;gap:32px"><div class="toc-blog" style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px;max-width:100%;position:relative;top:0"><div class="toc-blog__title" style="color:rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);font-family:Inter;font-size:12px;font-weight:400;line-height:18px;text-align:left">TABLE OF CONTENTS</div><div class="toc-blog__body" style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px"><a class="toc-blog__item" href="#Key_Findings" id="btn-Key_Findings" style="color:rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);font-family:Inter;font-size:12px;font-weight:400;line-height:18px;text-align:left;padding-left:12px;text-decoration:none;border-left:1px solid transparent">Key Findings</a><a class="toc-blog__item" href="#What_is_OpenClaw" id="btn-What_is_OpenClaw" style="color:rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);font-family:Inter;font-size:12px;font-weight:400;line-height:18px;text-align:left;padding-left:12px;text-decoration:none;border-left:1px solid transparent">What is OpenClaw?</a><a class="toc-blog__item" href="#Framework_Distribution" id="btn-Framework_Distribution" style="color:rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);font-family:Inter;font-size:12px;font-weight:400;line-height:18px;text-align:left;padding-left:12px;text-decoration:none;border-left:1px solid transparent">Framework Distribution</a><a class="toc-blog__item" href="#Hunting_Methodology" id="btn-Hunting_Methodology" style="color:rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);font-family:Inter;font-size:12px;font-weight:400;line-height:18px;text-align:left;padding-left:12px;text-decoration:none;border-left:1px solid transparent">Hunting Methodology</a><a class="toc-blog__item" href="#CVE202625253_Target_Ports" id="btn-CVE202625253_Target_Ports" style="color:rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);font-family:Inter;font-size:12px;font-weight:400;line-height:18px;text-align:left;padding-left:12px;text-decoration:none;border-left:1px solid transparent">CVE-2026-25253 Target Ports</a><a class="toc-blog__item" href="#Mitigation_Strategies" id="btn-Mitigation_Strategies" style="color:rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);font-family:Inter;font-size:12px;font-weight:400;line-height:18px;text-align:left;padding-left:12px;text-decoration:none;border-left:1px solid transparent">Mitigation Strategies</a><a class="toc-blog__item" href="#Conclusion" id="btn-Conclusion" style="color:rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);font-family:Inter;font-size:12px;font-weight:400;line-height:18px;text-align:left;padding-left:12px;text-decoration:none;border-left:1px solid transparent">Conclusion</a></div></div><div style="flex:1;max-width:100%;margin-right:0%"><div class="markdown" style="width:100%;overflow:hidden"><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">In reaction to the recent CVE-2026-25253, our research team set out to understand how this vulnerability appears in real-world deployments and what could be identified at internet scale using Hunt.io.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">Our analysis focused on internet-exposed browser automation frameworks affected by CVE-2026-25253, including OpenClaw and its forks Clawdbot and Moltbot, which expose web-based control interfaces that store API credentials for multiple AI services. When deployed without proper access controls, these interfaces are directly reachable from the public internet.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">In this report, our team outlines how exposed OpenClaw instances were identified at scale, the infrastructure patterns behind those deployments, and the detection techniques used to fingerprint vulnerable instances using Hunt.io's SQL-based search capabilities.</p><h2 id="Key_Findings" style="scroll-margin-top: 22vh; font-size: 22px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; line-height: 1.5; margin-top: 1.2em;">Key Findings</h2><ul style="color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
        margin: 21px 0;
        line-height: 1.5;
        color: black;
        margin-bottom: 4em;"><li style="margin-bottom: -1.6em;color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);"><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">Identified over 17,500 exposed instances of OpenClaw, Clawdbot, and Moltbot vulnerable to CVE-2026-25253.</p></li><li style="margin-bottom: -1.6em;color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);"><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">The vulnerability allows unauthenticated access to stored API tokens via the /api/export-auth endpoint.</p></li><li style="margin-bottom: -1.6em;color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);"><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">Affected instances store credentials for Claude, OpenAI, Google AI, and other AI services.</p></li><li style="margin-bottom: -1.6em;color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);"><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">Clawdbot Control represents 68.9% of identified deployments, followed by Moltbot Control (22.3%) and OpenClaw Control (8.8%).</p></li><li style="margin-bottom: -1.6em;color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);"><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">Exposed instances were observed across 52 countries, with the highest concentrations in the United States and China.</p></li><li style="margin-bottom: -1.6em;color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);"><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">98.6% of deployments run on cloud or hosting infrastructure, primarily DigitalOcean, Alibaba Cloud, and Tencent.</p></li><li style="margin-bottom: -1.6em;color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);"><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">While port 18789 is the most common, many instances also run on ports 80 and 443, often behind reverse proxies.</p></li><li style="margin-bottom: -1.6em;color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);"><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">Multiple detection methods were validated, including HTML title matching, HTTP header hashing, body hashing, asset fingerprinting, TLS certificate fingerprints, and content-length analysis.</p></li></ul>
<p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">To provide context for these findings, the following section outlines what OpenClaw is and how its control interface is typically deployed.</p><h2 id="What_is_OpenClaw" style="scroll-margin-top: 22vh; font-size: 22px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; line-height: 1.5; margin-top: 1.2em;">What is OpenClaw?</h2><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         "><a href="https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="
            font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
            color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
            text-decoration: underline;" class="link-hover">OpenClaw</a> (and its popular forks Clawdbot and Moltbot) is an open-source browser automation framework designed to connect large language models like Claude and GPT-4 to web browsers through the Playwright automation library. Users configure the framework with their API keys to enable AI agents to autonomously browse websites, fill out forms, extract data, and perform complex web-based tasks.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">The framework exposes a web-based control panel typically accessible on port 18789 that provides a graphical interface for managing automation sessions, viewing real-time browser activity, configuring AI model parameters, and storing authentication credentials. This control panel is the primary attack surface for CVE-2026-25253.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         "><strong>The Vulnerability</strong>: <a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-25253" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="
            font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
            color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
            text-decoration: underline;" class="link-hover">CVE-2026-25253</a> affects all versions of OpenClaw before the security patch. The /api/export-auth endpoint was designed to allow users to back up their stored credentials, but the endpoint lacks any authentication or authorization checks. This means anyone who can reach the service over the network can extract all stored API tokens, including keys for Claude, OpenAI, Google AI, and other services.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">To assess the scope of this exposure, our research team analyzed how OpenClaw and its forks are distributed across deployed variants.</p><h2 id="Framework_Distribution" style="scroll-margin-top: 22vh; font-size: 22px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; line-height: 1.5; margin-top: 1.2em;">Framework Distribution</h2><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">The affected software ecosystem consists of three related browser automation frameworks sharing a common codebase derived from the original OpenClaw project.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">The analysis of 17,470 instances with identifiable HTML titles reveals that Clawdbot Control is the dominant variant at 68.9% of all deployments, followed by Moltbot Control at 22.3% and the original OpenClaw Control at 8.8%.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">From a threat hunting perspective, this means detection rules must account for all three title variants to achieve comprehensive coverage. The prevalence of Clawdbot suggests it has become the most actively maintained fork, which may also make it the most likely to receive a timely security patch for CVE-2026-25253.</p><img src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Article_img_Framework+Distribution+17%2C470+Total+Instances.png" alt="Figure 1: Framework Distribution Across 17,470 Identified Instances" title class="markdown-image" data-src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Article_img_Framework+Distribution+17%2C470+Total+Instances.png" data-alt="Figure 1: Framework Distribution Across 17,470 Identified Instances" style="
                display: block;
                margin: 1em auto -1em auto;
                max-width: 85%;
                max-height: 600px;
                cursor: zoom-in;
                overflow: hidden;
                border-radius: 6px;
                box-shadow: -1px 4px 23px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);"><span class="figure" style="
                font-size: 14px;
                color: rgb(255, 255, 255);
                font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
                line-height: 1.5;
                margin-top: 1.2em;
                text-align: center;
                font-weight: 400;
                margin-bottom: 2em;
                display: block;
                font-style: italic;
                margin-inline: auto;
                max-width: 80%;
            ">Figure 1: Framework Distribution Across 17,470 Identified Instances</span><h3 style="font-size: 16px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; line-height: 1.5; margin-top: 1.2em;">Geographic Distribution</h3><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">Using <a href="https://hunt.io/features/bulk-enrichment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="
            font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
            color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
            text-decoration: underline;" class="link-hover">Hunt.io Bulk IP Enrichment</a> of 2,500 unique IP addresses through geolocation and ASN analysis reveals the global spread of vulnerable OpenClaw deployments across 52 countries.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">The United States leads with 891 instances (35.6%), driven primarily by DigitalOcean data centers in Santa Clara and Clifton. China follows with 648 instances (25.9%), concentrated across Alibaba Cloud and Tencent infrastructure in Beijing, Hangzhou, and Shanghai.</p><img src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Hunting+OpenClaw+Exposures+CVE-2026-25253+in+Internet-Facing+AI+Agent+Gateways+-+figure+2.png" alt="Figure 2: Bulk extraction and enrichment results showing 2,500 unique IPv4 addresses" title class="markdown-image" data-src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Hunting+OpenClaw+Exposures+CVE-2026-25253+in+Internet-Facing+AI+Agent+Gateways+-+figure+2.png" data-alt="Figure 2: Bulk extraction and enrichment results showing 2,500 unique IPv4 addresses" style="
                display: block;
                margin: 1em auto -1em auto;
                max-width: 85%;
                max-height: 600px;
                cursor: zoom-in;
                overflow: hidden;
                border-radius: 6px;
                box-shadow: -1px 4px 23px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);"><span class="figure" style="
                font-size: 14px;
                color: rgb(255, 255, 255);
                font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
                line-height: 1.5;
                margin-top: 1.2em;
                text-align: center;
                font-weight: 400;
                margin-bottom: 2em;
                display: block;
                font-style: italic;
                margin-inline: auto;
                max-width: 80%;
            ">Figure 2: Bulk extraction and enrichment results showing 2,500 unique IPv4 addresses</span><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">European and Asian hosting hubs account for the remaining deployments, with Germany (6.6%), Singapore (6.5%), and Hong Kong (4.4%) serving as significant regional nodes.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">This concentration aligns with how browser automation workloads are typically deployed, requiring persistent compute resources and stable network connectivity.</p><img src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Article_img_Geographic+Distribution+of+Exposed+Instances+(%D0%A2%D0%BE%D1%80+10+Countries).png" alt="Figure 3: Geographic Distribution of Exposed Instances by Country (Top 10)" title class="markdown-image" data-src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Article_img_Geographic+Distribution+of+Exposed+Instances+(%D0%A2%D0%BE%D1%80+10+Countries).png" data-alt="Figure 3: Geographic Distribution of Exposed Instances by Country (Top 10)" style="
                display: block;
                margin: 1em auto -1em auto;
                max-width: 85%;
                max-height: 600px;
                cursor: zoom-in;
                overflow: hidden;
                border-radius: 6px;
                box-shadow: -1px 4px 23px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);"><span class="figure" style="
                font-size: 14px;
                color: rgb(255, 255, 255);
                font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
                line-height: 1.5;
                margin-top: 1.2em;
                text-align: center;
                font-weight: 400;
                margin-bottom: 2em;
                display: block;
                font-style: italic;
                margin-inline: auto;
                max-width: 80%;
            ">Figure 3: Geographic Distribution of Exposed Instances by Country (Top 10)</span><h3 style="font-size: 16px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; line-height: 1.5; margin-top: 1.2em;">Top Cities</h3><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">City-level analysis reveals that exposed instances cluster around major cloud provider data center locations rather than traditional enterprise hubs. Santa Clara leads with 250 instances corresponding to DigitalOcean and cloud infrastructure in Silicon Valley, while Clifton, New Jersey, accounts for 207 instances on East Coast hosting platforms. In China, Beijing (214), Hangzhou (121), Shanghai (119), and Guangzhou (97) collectively represent substantial Alibaba Cloud and Tencent deployments.</p><img src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Article_img_Top+Cities+by+Exposure+(Enriched+IP+Analysis).png" alt="Figure 4: Top 10 Cities Hosting Vulnerable OpenClaw Instances" title class="markdown-image" data-src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Article_img_Top+Cities+by+Exposure+(Enriched+IP+Analysis).png" data-alt="Figure 4: Top 10 Cities Hosting Vulnerable OpenClaw Instances" style="
                display: block;
                margin: 1em auto -1em auto;
                max-width: 85%;
                max-height: 600px;
                cursor: zoom-in;
                overflow: hidden;
                border-radius: 6px;
                box-shadow: -1px 4px 23px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);"><span class="figure" style="
                font-size: 14px;
                color: rgb(255, 255, 255);
                font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
                line-height: 1.5;
                margin-top: 1.2em;
                text-align: center;
                font-weight: 400;
                margin-bottom: 2em;
                display: block;
                font-style: italic;
                margin-inline: auto;
                max-width: 80%;
            ">Figure 4: Top 10 Cities Hosting Vulnerable OpenClaw Instances</span><h3 style="font-size: 16px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; line-height: 1.5; margin-top: 1.2em;">Infrastructure Providers</h3><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">ASN analysis confirms that 98.6% of exposed instances run on commercial hosting and cloud infrastructure, with only 20 instances on ISP networks and 16 on business-class connections. DigitalOcean is the single largest host with 880 instances (35.2%), followed by Alibaba Cloud entities collectively hosting 660 instances (26.4%) across their Hangzhou and US-based data centers. Tencent rounds out the top three with 307 combined instances (12.3%).</p><img src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Article_img_Infrastructure+Providers+(Top+10+by+Instance+Count).png" alt="Figure 5: Top 10 Infrastructure Providers Hosting Vulnerable Instances" title class="markdown-image" data-src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Article_img_Infrastructure+Providers+(Top+10+by+Instance+Count).png" data-alt="Figure 5: Top 10 Infrastructure Providers Hosting Vulnerable Instances" style="
                display: block;
                margin: 1em auto -1em auto;
                max-width: 85%;
                max-height: 600px;
                cursor: zoom-in;
                overflow: hidden;
                border-radius: 6px;
                box-shadow: -1px 4px 23px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);"><span class="figure" style="
                font-size: 14px;
                color: rgb(255, 255, 255);
                font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
                line-height: 1.5;
                margin-top: 1.2em;
                text-align: center;
                font-weight: 400;
                margin-bottom: 2em;
                display: block;
                font-style: italic;
                margin-inline: auto;
                max-width: 80%;
            ">Figure 5: Top 10 Infrastructure Providers Hosting Vulnerable Instances</span><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">The dominance of cloud hosting providers indicates that most OpenClaw deployments are intentional server-side installations rather than accidental exposures from desktop applications. This also means that cloud provider security teams and abuse departments could play a significant role in driving remediation by notifying affected customers of the exposed vulnerability.</p><img src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Article_img_ASN+Type+Distribution.png" alt="Figure 6: ASN Type Distribution - 98.6% Hosted on Cloud/Hosting Infrastructure" title class="markdown-image" data-src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Article_img_ASN+Type+Distribution.png" data-alt="Figure 6: ASN Type Distribution - 98.6% Hosted on Cloud/Hosting Infrastructure" style="
                display: block;
                margin: 1em auto -1em auto;
                max-width: 85%;
                max-height: 600px;
                cursor: zoom-in;
                overflow: hidden;
                border-radius: 6px;
                box-shadow: -1px 4px 23px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);"><span class="figure" style="
                font-size: 14px;
                color: rgb(255, 255, 255);
                font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
                line-height: 1.5;
                margin-top: 1.2em;
                text-align: center;
                font-weight: 400;
                margin-bottom: 2em;
                display: block;
                font-style: italic;
                margin-inline: auto;
                max-width: 80%;
            ">Figure 6: ASN Type Distribution - 98.6% Hosted on Cloud/Hosting Infrastructure</span><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">Beyond hosting providers, port usage patterns provide additional insight into how these instances are exposed and accessed.</p><h3 style="font-size: 16px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; line-height: 1.5; margin-top: 1.2em;">Port Distribution</h3><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">Port analysis across all 17,470 identified instances reveals that while the default port 18789 accounts for the largest share at 47.3% (8,258 instances), a substantial number of deployments run on standard web ports. HTTP port 80 hosts 30.1% (5,263 instances) and HTTPS port 443 hosts 18.9% (3,299 instances), indicating widespread use of reverse proxies like nginx to front the application.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">The presence of instances on non-standard ports such as 18888 (243 instances) and 8080 (100 instances) suggests custom deployment configurations that may require additional hunting rules. Defenders should note that port-filtered queries targeting only 18789 and 18791 will miss approximately half of all exposed instances, making title-based and body-hash detection methods essential for comprehensive coverage.</p><img src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Article_img_Port+Distribution.png" alt="Figure 7: Port Distribution Across All Identified Instances" title class="markdown-image" data-src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Article_img_Port+Distribution.png" data-alt="Figure 7: Port Distribution Across All Identified Instances" style="
                display: block;
                margin: 1em auto -1em auto;
                max-width: 85%;
                max-height: 600px;
                cursor: zoom-in;
                overflow: hidden;
                border-radius: 6px;
                box-shadow: -1px 4px 23px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);"><span class="figure" style="
                font-size: 14px;
                color: rgb(255, 255, 255);
                font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
                line-height: 1.5;
                margin-top: 1.2em;
                text-align: center;
                font-weight: 400;
                margin-bottom: 2em;
                display: block;
                font-style: italic;
                margin-inline: auto;
                max-width: 80%;
            ">Figure 7: Port Distribution Across All Identified Instances</span><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">Based on these exposure patterns, our research team developed multiple detection strategies to identify vulnerable OpenClaw instances at scale.</p><h2 id="Hunting_Methodology" style="scroll-margin-top: 22vh; font-size: 22px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; line-height: 1.5; margin-top: 1.2em;">Hunting Methodology</h2><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">Using <a href="https://hunt.io/features/hunt-sql" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="
            font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
            color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
            text-decoration: underline;" class="link-hover">Hunt.io's powerful SQL-based search</a> against our comprehensive internet scanning database, we analyzed exposed OpenClaw instances across the global internet.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">The following sections document each fingerprinting technique with the exact HuntSQL queries used, along with screenshots showing real-world results. Security teams can adapt these queries for their own threat hunting operations to identify potentially vulnerable assets within their network perimeter or among their cloud infrastructure.</p><h3 style="font-size: 16px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; line-height: 1.5; margin-top: 1.2em;">Detection Strategy 1: HTML Title Matching</h3><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">The most straightforward and reliable fingerprint for identifying OpenClaw instances is the HTML page title returned by the web control panel. Each fork of the framework uses a distinctive title that clearly identifies the variant: "Clawdbot Control" for the original framework, "Moltbot Control" for the Chinese fork, and "OpenClaw Control" for the community rebrand. These titles remain consistent across deployments and are rarely modified by operators.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">We tested two query approaches for maximum coverage. The first uses a simple LIKE pattern matching with wildcards to catch each variant separately.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         "><strong>Query (LIKE method):</strong></p><pre><code class="language-sql"><code class=" language-sql" data-raw="SELECT ip, port, hostname, html.head.title
FROM httpv2
WHERE html.head.title LIKE '%Clawdbot Control%'
   OR html.head.title LIKE '%OpenClaw Control%'
   OR html.head.title LIKE '%Moltbot Control%'
"><span class="hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> ip, port, hostname, html.head.title
<span class="hljs-keyword">FROM</span> httpv2
<span class="hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> html.head.title <span class="hljs-keyword">LIKE</span> <span class="hljs-string">'%Clawdbot Control%'</span>
   <span class="hljs-keyword">OR</span> html.head.title <span class="hljs-keyword">LIKE</span> <span class="hljs-string">'%OpenClaw Control%'</span>
   <span class="hljs-keyword">OR</span> html.head.title <span class="hljs-keyword">LIKE</span> <span class="hljs-string">'%Moltbot Control%'</span>
</code>
                <div class="code-actions"><div class="copy-trigger" style="width: 80px;
                    padding: unset !important;
                    display: flex;
                    justify-content: center;
                    align-items: center;
                    text-align: center;
                    border-radius: 4px;
                    border: unset;
                    height: 30px;
                    box-sizing: border-box;
                    cursor: pointer;
                    gap: 4px;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-copy-icon lucide-copy"><rect width="14" height="14" x="8" y="8" rx="2" ry="2"/><path d="M4 16c-1.1 0-2-.9-2-2V4c0-1.1.9-2 2-2h10c1.1 0 2 .9 2 2"/></svg><span>Copy</span></div></div></code></pre>
<p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">Sample output:</p><img src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Hunting+OpenClaw+Exposures+CVE-2026-25253+in+Internet-Facing+AI+Agent+Gateways+-+figure+8.png" alt="Figure 8: HTML Title Search using LIKE - 17,587 Results" title class="markdown-image" data-src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Hunting+OpenClaw+Exposures+CVE-2026-25253+in+Internet-Facing+AI+Agent+Gateways+-+figure+8.png" data-alt="Figure 8: HTML Title Search using LIKE - 17,587 Results" style="
                display: block;
                margin: 1em auto -1em auto;
                max-width: 85%;
                max-height: 600px;
                cursor: zoom-in;
                overflow: hidden;
                border-radius: 6px;
                box-shadow: -1px 4px 23px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);"><span class="figure" style="
                font-size: 14px;
                color: rgb(255, 255, 255);
                font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
                line-height: 1.5;
                margin-top: 1.2em;
                text-align: center;
                font-weight: 400;
                margin-bottom: 2em;
                display: block;
                font-style: italic;
                margin-inline: auto;
                max-width: 80%;
            ">Figure 8: HTML Title Search using LIKE - 17,587 Results</span><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">The second uses RLIKE with a case-insensitive regular expression to capture all three variants in a single query.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         "><strong>Query (RLIKE regex method):</strong></p><pre><code class="language-sql"><code class=" language-sql" data-raw="SELECT ip, port, hostname, html.head.title
FROM httpv2
WHERE html.head.title RLIKE '(?i)(clawdbot|moltbot|openclaw).*control'
"><span class="hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> ip, port, hostname, html.head.title
<span class="hljs-keyword">FROM</span> httpv2
<span class="hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> html.head.title RLIKE <span class="hljs-string">'(?i)(clawdbot|moltbot|openclaw).*control'</span>
</code>
                <div class="code-actions"><div class="copy-trigger" style="width: 80px;
                    padding: unset !important;
                    display: flex;
                    justify-content: center;
                    align-items: center;
                    text-align: center;
                    border-radius: 4px;
                    border: unset;
                    height: 30px;
                    box-sizing: border-box;
                    cursor: pointer;
                    gap: 4px;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-copy-icon lucide-copy"><rect width="14" height="14" x="8" y="8" rx="2" ry="2"/><path d="M4 16c-1.1 0-2-.9-2-2V4c0-1.1.9-2 2-2h10c1.1 0 2 .9 2 2"/></svg><span>Copy</span></div></div></code></pre>
<p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">Output example:</p><img src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Hunting+OpenClaw+Exposures+CVE-2026-25253+in+Internet-Facing+AI+Agent+Gateways+-+figure+9.png" alt="Figure 9: HTML Title Search using RLIKE (Regex) - 17,590 Results" title class="markdown-image" data-src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Hunting+OpenClaw+Exposures+CVE-2026-25253+in+Internet-Facing+AI+Agent+Gateways+-+figure+9.png" data-alt="Figure 9: HTML Title Search using RLIKE (Regex) - 17,590 Results" style="
                display: block;
                margin: 1em auto -1em auto;
                max-width: 85%;
                max-height: 600px;
                cursor: zoom-in;
                overflow: hidden;
                border-radius: 6px;
                box-shadow: -1px 4px 23px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);"><span class="figure" style="
                font-size: 14px;
                color: rgb(255, 255, 255);
                font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
                line-height: 1.5;
                margin-top: 1.2em;
                text-align: center;
                font-weight: 400;
                margin-bottom: 2em;
                display: block;
                font-style: italic;
                margin-inline: auto;
                max-width: 80%;
            ">Figure 9: HTML Title Search using RLIKE (Regex) - 17,590 Results</span><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">Both approaches proved highly effective, with the regex query returning slightly more results due to its ability to match variations in capitalization and spacing.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">This method stands out because it requires no prior knowledge of specific hash values or fingerprints - it directly identifies the application by its self-declared identity. The results show exposed instances running on a wide variety of ports beyond the default, indicating that many operators attempt security through obscurity by changing the listening port.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">The regex query captured 17,590 instances across all three variants. Results reveal instances running on various non-standard ports, including 80, 443, 8080, 8081, 9009, 18888, and the default 18789, demonstrating that port-based filtering alone is insufficient for comprehensive detection.</p><h3 style="font-size: 16px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; line-height: 1.5; margin-top: 1.2em;">Detection Strategy 2: HTTP Header Fingerprinting</h3><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">HTTP response headers provide another reliable fingerprinting vector because web servers and application frameworks produce consistent header patterns. OpenClaw instances share characteristic header combinations due to their common Vite-based frontend build system and Express.js backend.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">By computing Murmur3 hashes of the response headers, we can efficiently search for matching patterns across billions of HTTP responses in our database.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">We tested three different header hash variants to understand their effectiveness: normalized headers (standardized ordering and formatting), sorted headers (alphabetically ordered), and unique headers (deduplicated header names). Each variant captures slightly different aspects of the HTTP response signature, but all three converge on the same set of approximately 8,260 instances when filtered to the default port.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">The consistency across all three hash types validates the reliability of header-based fingerprinting for this target. The hash values were extracted from confirmed OpenClaw instances during our initial reconnaissance phase, then used as search signatures to discover additional exposed deployments at scale.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">The following query matches normalized HTTP header hashes extracted from confirmed OpenClaw instances.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         "><strong>Query (Normalized Header Hash):</strong></p><pre><code class="language-sql"><code class=" language-sql" data-raw="SELECT ip, port, hostname, html.head.title
FROM httpv2
WHERE http.headers.normalized.hash.murmur3 IN (
    '01D737B6D4249593',
    'A21D8F4148A7091A',
    'B5B27B39C533B437',
    '75530A6460E2396C',
    '21DFA63A75ACFD53',
    'D263AF8ACFBCB72B',
    'A905FF566F28EC67',
    '2227E50E61F4757E',
    '91794924F783020C',
    'D417839C4651FBDE'
) AND port IN (18789, 18791)
"><span class="hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> ip, port, hostname, html.head.title
<span class="hljs-keyword">FROM</span> httpv2
<span class="hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> http.headers.normalized.hash.murmur3 <span class="hljs-keyword">IN</span> (
    <span class="hljs-string">'01D737B6D4249593'</span>,
    <span class="hljs-string">'A21D8F4148A7091A'</span>,
    <span class="hljs-string">'B5B27B39C533B437'</span>,
    <span class="hljs-string">'75530A6460E2396C'</span>,
    <span class="hljs-string">'21DFA63A75ACFD53'</span>,
    <span class="hljs-string">'D263AF8ACFBCB72B'</span>,
    <span class="hljs-string">'A905FF566F28EC67'</span>,
    <span class="hljs-string">'2227E50E61F4757E'</span>,
    <span class="hljs-string">'91794924F783020C'</span>,
    <span class="hljs-string">'D417839C4651FBDE'</span>
) <span class="hljs-keyword">AND</span> port <span class="hljs-keyword">IN</span> (<span class="hljs-number">18789</span>, <span class="hljs-number">18791</span>)
</code>
                <div class="code-actions"><div class="copy-trigger" style="width: 80px;
                    padding: unset !important;
                    display: flex;
                    justify-content: center;
                    align-items: center;
                    text-align: center;
