<p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">Output example:</p><img src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Hunting+OpenClaw+Exposures+CVE-2026-25253+in+Internet-Facing+AI+Agent+Gateways+-+figure+17.png" alt="Figure 17: CSS Asset Fingerprints with Port Filter - 8,206 Results" title class="markdown-image" data-src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Hunting+OpenClaw+Exposures+CVE-2026-25253+in+Internet-Facing+AI+Agent+Gateways+-+figure+17.png" data-alt="Figure 17: CSS Asset Fingerprints with Port Filter - 8,206 Results" style="
                display: block;
                margin: 1em auto -1em auto;
                max-width: 85%;
                max-height: 600px;
                cursor: zoom-in;
                overflow: hidden;
                border-radius: 6px;
                box-shadow: -1px 4px 23px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);"><span class="figure" style="
                font-size: 14px;
                color: rgb(255, 255, 255);
                font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
                line-height: 1.5;
                margin-top: 1.2em;
                text-align: center;
                font-weight: 400;
                margin-bottom: 2em;
                display: block;
                font-style: italic;
                margin-inline: auto;
                max-width: 80%;
            ">Figure 17: CSS Asset Fingerprints with Port Filter - 8,206 Results</span><h3 style="font-size: 16px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; line-height: 1.5; margin-top: 1.2em;">Detection Strategy 6: TLS Certificate Fingerprints (JA4X)</h3><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">JA4X is a TLS certificate fingerprinting method that creates unique identifiers based on certificate properties including the issuer, subject, and extension information. For OpenClaw instances configured with TLS encryption, these certificate fingerprints provide a reliable identification mechanism that operates at the transport layer, independent of any HTTP-level characteristics.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">Our analysis revealed that many OpenClaw deployments use self-signed certificates with distinctive organization names such as "Clawdbot", "Moltbot", or "OpenClaw" in the certificate fields. Others use certificates from common providers like Let's Encrypt, ZeroSSL, or Cloudflare. The JA4X fingerprints capture these patterns, enabling detection even when the HTTP response content has been modified or is unavailable.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">This detection method found 189 instances with TLS certificates, representing the subset of deployments that have enabled HTTPS. While this is a smaller population than HTTP-only instances, TLS-enabled deployments represent a smaller subset of exposed instances and can be prioritized separately during remediation efforts.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">The following query uses JA4X certificate fingerprints to identify TLS-enabled OpenClaw deployments at the transport layer.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         "><strong>Query:</strong></p><pre><code class="language-sql"><code class=" language-sql" data-raw="SELECT ip, port, hostname, html.head.title
FROM httpv2
WHERE tls.cert.hash.ja4x IN (
    'a373a9f83c6b_000000000000_25294184ae24',
    'a373a9f83c6b_7022c563de38_2e3757343cb0',
    '7022c563de38_7022c563de38_795797892f9c',
    '2bab15409345_2bab15409345_795797892f9c',
    'a373a9f83c6b_7022c563de38_f64f03ad387e',
    '7d5dbb3783b4_7022c563de38_f64f03ad387e',
    '2166164053c1_2166164053c1_795797892f9c',
    'a373a9f83c6b_7022c563de38_2cdf432ec278',
    'dc020972a4a8_9fb583da09a2_fb02ba79e164'
)
AND port IN (18789, 18791)
"><span class="hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> ip, port, hostname, html.head.title
<span class="hljs-keyword">FROM</span> httpv2
<span class="hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> tls.cert.hash.ja4x <span class="hljs-keyword">IN</span> (
    <span class="hljs-string">'a373a9f83c6b_000000000000_25294184ae24'</span>,
    <span class="hljs-string">'a373a9f83c6b_7022c563de38_2e3757343cb0'</span>,
    <span class="hljs-string">'7022c563de38_7022c563de38_795797892f9c'</span>,
    <span class="hljs-string">'2bab15409345_2bab15409345_795797892f9c'</span>,
    <span class="hljs-string">'a373a9f83c6b_7022c563de38_f64f03ad387e'</span>,
    <span class="hljs-string">'7d5dbb3783b4_7022c563de38_f64f03ad387e'</span>,
    <span class="hljs-string">'2166164053c1_2166164053c1_795797892f9c'</span>,
    <span class="hljs-string">'a373a9f83c6b_7022c563de38_2cdf432ec278'</span>,
    <span class="hljs-string">'dc020972a4a8_9fb583da09a2_fb02ba79e164'</span>
)
<span class="hljs-keyword">AND</span> port <span class="hljs-keyword">IN</span> (<span class="hljs-number">18789</span>, <span class="hljs-number">18791</span>)
</code>
                <div class="code-actions"><div class="copy-trigger" style="width: 80px;
                    padding: unset !important;
                    display: flex;
                    justify-content: center;
                    align-items: center;
                    text-align: center;
                    border-radius: 4px;
                    border: unset;
                    height: 30px;
                    box-sizing: border-box;
                    cursor: pointer;
                    gap: 4px;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-copy-icon lucide-copy"><rect width="14" height="14" x="8" y="8" rx="2" ry="2"/><path d="M4 16c-1.1 0-2-.9-2-2V4c0-1.1.9-2 2-2h10c1.1 0 2 .9 2 2"/></svg><span>Copy</span></div></div></code></pre>
<p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">Output example:</p><img src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Hunting+OpenClaw+Exposures+CVE-2026-25253+in+Internet-Facing+AI+Agent+Gateways+-+figure+18.png" alt="Figure 18: JA4X TLS Certificate Fingerprints - 189 Results" title class="markdown-image" data-src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Hunting+OpenClaw+Exposures+CVE-2026-25253+in+Internet-Facing+AI+Agent+Gateways+-+figure+18.png" data-alt="Figure 18: JA4X TLS Certificate Fingerprints - 189 Results" style="
                display: block;
                margin: 1em auto -1em auto;
                max-width: 85%;
                max-height: 600px;
                cursor: zoom-in;
                overflow: hidden;
                border-radius: 6px;
                box-shadow: -1px 4px 23px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);"><span class="figure" style="
                font-size: 14px;
                color: rgb(255, 255, 255);
                font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
                line-height: 1.5;
                margin-top: 1.2em;
                text-align: center;
                font-weight: 400;
                margin-bottom: 2em;
                display: block;
                font-style: italic;
                margin-inline: auto;
                max-width: 80%;
            ">Figure 18: JA4X TLS Certificate Fingerprints - 189 Results</span><h3 style="font-size: 16px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; line-height: 1.5; margin-top: 1.2em;">Detection Strategy 7: Content-Length Header</h3><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">The HTTP Content-Length header indicates the size of the response body in bytes. For web applications with static index pages like OpenClaw, this value remains consistent across deployments of the same version. The framework's default landing page produces response bodies of characteristic sizes: 671 bytes, 668 bytes, 841 bytes, 670 bytes, 669 bytes, and 667 bytes, depending on the specific version and configuration.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">While Content-Length alone is not a unique identifier (many applications might produce responses of similar sizes), when combined with port filtering for the CVE-specific ports 18789 and 18791, this simple fingerprint becomes highly effective. The query identified 7,619 instances, demonstrating that even basic HTTP metadata can be valuable for threat hunting when properly contextualized.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">This detection method is particularly useful as a quick validation check or for environments where more sophisticated fingerprinting data may not be available. It can also serve as a first-pass filter to reduce the dataset before applying more computationally expensive analysis techniques.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">This method relies on consistent Content-Length values observed across OpenClaw versions when combined with port filtering.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         "><strong>Query:</strong></p><pre><code class="language-sql"><code class=" language-sql" data-raw="SELECT ip, port, hostname, html.head.title
FROM httpv2
WHERE http.headers.content_length IN ('671', '668', '841', '670', '669', '667')
AND port IN (18789, 18791)
"><span class="hljs-keyword">SELECT</span> ip, port, hostname, html.head.title
<span class="hljs-keyword">FROM</span> httpv2
<span class="hljs-keyword">WHERE</span> http.headers.content_length <span class="hljs-keyword">IN</span> (<span class="hljs-string">'671'</span>, <span class="hljs-string">'668'</span>, <span class="hljs-string">'841'</span>, <span class="hljs-string">'670'</span>, <span class="hljs-string">'669'</span>, <span class="hljs-string">'667'</span>)
<span class="hljs-keyword">AND</span> port <span class="hljs-keyword">IN</span> (<span class="hljs-number">18789</span>, <span class="hljs-number">18791</span>)
</code>
                <div class="code-actions"><div class="copy-trigger" style="width: 80px;
                    padding: unset !important;
                    display: flex;
                    justify-content: center;
                    align-items: center;
                    text-align: center;
                    border-radius: 4px;
                    border: unset;
                    height: 30px;
                    box-sizing: border-box;
                    cursor: pointer;
                    gap: 4px;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-copy-icon lucide-copy"><rect width="14" height="14" x="8" y="8" rx="2" ry="2"/><path d="M4 16c-1.1 0-2-.9-2-2V4c0-1.1.9-2 2-2h10c1.1 0 2 .9 2 2"/></svg><span>Copy</span></div></div></code></pre>
<p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">Output example:</p><img src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Hunting+OpenClaw+Exposures+CVE-2026-25253+in+Internet-Facing+AI+Agent+Gateways+-+figure+19.png" alt="Figure 19: Content-Length Header with Port Filter - 7,619 Results" title class="markdown-image" data-src="https://public-hunt-static-blog-assets.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/2-2026/Hunting+OpenClaw+Exposures+CVE-2026-25253+in+Internet-Facing+AI+Agent+Gateways+-+figure+19.png" data-alt="Figure 19: Content-Length Header with Port Filter - 7,619 Results" style="
                display: block;
                margin: 1em auto -1em auto;
                max-width: 85%;
                max-height: 600px;
                cursor: zoom-in;
                overflow: hidden;
                border-radius: 6px;
                box-shadow: -1px 4px 23px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);"><span class="figure" style="
                font-size: 14px;
                color: rgb(255, 255, 255);
                font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
                line-height: 1.5;
                margin-top: 1.2em;
                text-align: center;
                font-weight: 400;
                margin-bottom: 2em;
                display: block;
                font-style: italic;
                margin-inline: auto;
                max-width: 80%;
            ">Figure 19: Content-Length Header with Port Filter - 7,619 Results</span><h2 id="CVE202625253_Target_Ports" style="scroll-margin-top: 22vh; font-size: 22px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; line-height: 1.5; margin-top: 1.2em;">CVE-2026-25253 Target Ports</h2><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">The vulnerability specifically affects services running on the default OpenClaw ports documented in the CVE advisory. Understanding the role of each port is essential for both detection and remediation efforts. Port 18789 hosts the main gateway API where the vulnerable authentication export endpoint resides, making it the primary target for exploitation.</p><div style="width: 100%; overflow: auto;"><table style="
            border-radius: 12px;
            overflow: hidden;
            border: 0.5px solid #E6E8EC;
            border-spacing: 0;
            width:100%;
            color: rgb(255, 255, 255);
            line-height: 1.5;
            text-align: left;
            min-width: 100%;
            margin-bottom: 24px;
            margin-top: 12px;
            font-family: Inter;"><thead style="background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);color: rgb(0, 0, 0);"><tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd;"><th style="padding: 14px 16px; font-weight: bold;">Port</th><th style="padding: 14px 16px; font-weight: bold;">Service</th><th style="padding: 14px 16px; font-weight: bold;">Description</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd;"><td style="
            padding: 14px 16px;
            border: 0.5px solid #E6E8EC;
        ">18789</td><td style="
            padding: 14px 16px;
            border: 0.5px solid #E6E8EC;
        ">Gateway API</td><td style="
            padding: 14px 16px;
            border: 0.5px solid #E6E8EC;
        ">Primary CVE target - /api/export-auth endpoint</td></tr>
<tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd;"><td style="
            padding: 14px 16px;
            border: 0.5px solid #E6E8EC;
        ">18791</td><td style="
            padding: 14px 16px;
            border: 0.5px solid #E6E8EC;
        ">Browser Control</td><td style="
            padding: 14px 16px;
            border: 0.5px solid #E6E8EC;
        ">Playwright browser automation interface</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div><h2 id="Mitigation_Strategies" style="scroll-margin-top: 22vh; font-size: 22px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; line-height: 1.5; margin-top: 1.2em;">Mitigation Strategies</h2><ol style="color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
        margin: 21px 0;
        line-height: 1.5;
        color: black;
        margin-bottom: 4em;"><li style="margin-bottom: -1.6em;color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);"><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         "><strong>Immediate Network Isolation</strong>: Organizations running OpenClaw, Clawdbot, or Moltbot must immediately restrict network access to ports 18789 and 18791. These services should never be directly exposed to the public internet under any circumstances. Implement firewall rules or network security groups to block inbound connections from untrusted sources.</p></li><li style="margin-bottom: -1.6em;color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);"><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         "><strong>Credential Rotation</strong>: Assume that all API keys and authentication tokens stored in any exposed instance have been compromised. Immediately rotate credentials for Claude, OpenAI, Google AI, and any other AI services that were configured in the framework. Review API usage logs for signs of unauthorized access or unusual activity patterns.</p></li><li style="margin-bottom: -1.6em;color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);"><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         "><strong>Network Architecture Review</strong>: Never expose browser automation frameworks or AI agent tools directly to the internet. Implement defense-in-depth using VPNs, bastion hosts, zero-trust network access (ZTNA) solutions, or private network segments for remote administration. Consider using reverse proxies with authentication requirements.</p></li><li style="margin-bottom: -1.6em;color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);"><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         "><strong>Patch Management</strong>: Apply security patches as soon as they become available from the framework maintainers. Monitor the project repositories and security advisories for updates. Consider implementing automated patch management for development tools and internal applications.</p></li></ol>
<h2 id="Conclusion" style="scroll-margin-top: 22vh; font-size: 22px; color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; line-height: 1.5; margin-top: 1.2em;">Conclusion</h2><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">The number of exposed OpenClaw instances identified across the public internet shows the risk of deploying AI automation tools without proper access controls. With over 17,500 exposed instances identified through our scanning efforts, this vulnerability represents a substantial attack surface that could enable mass credential theft, affecting AI service providers and their customers worldwide.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">The multi-layered detection methodology presented in this report provides security teams with practical, battle-tested queries for identifying vulnerable assets. By combining HTML title matching, header fingerprinting, body hashing, asset identification, certificate analysis, and content-length filtering, organizations can achieve comprehensive coverage for OpenClaw detection across their network perimeter and cloud infrastructure.</p><p style="
             font-size: 14px;
             color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
             line-height: 1.5;
             margin-top: 1.2em;
             margin-bottom: 2em;
             word-wrap: break-word;
         ">If you want to explore how Hunt.io can be used to track similar exposure patterns or validate findings across your own attack surface, you can <a href="https://hunt.io/get-started" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="
            font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;
            color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
            text-decoration: underline;" class="link-hover">book a demo</a> to walk through the workflows used in this research.</p></div></div></div></div><!--/$--></div><!--/$--></div></div><div class="framer-w0hx3"><div class="framer-1841d7c"><div class="framer-pyb0dc" data-framer-name="Frame 2147238655"><div class="framer-1fwwqgy" data-framer-name="Frame 2147238503"><div class="ssr-variant hidden-14od9f9 hidden-rsfvs"><div class="framer-us3gxf" data-framer-name="Related Post" data-framer-component-type="RichTextContainer" style="justify-content:center;transform:none"><p dir="auto" style="--font-selector:R0Y7SW50ZXIgVGlnaHQtNjAw;--framer-font-family:&quot;Inter Tight&quot;, &quot;Inter Tight Placeholder&quot;, sans-serif;--framer-font-size:64px;--framer-font-weight:600;--framer-line-height:100%;--framer-text-color:rgb(255, 255, 255)" class="framer-text">Related <span style="--font-selector:R0Y7SW50ZXIgVGlnaHQtMzAw;--framer-font-weight:300" class="framer-text">Posts</span></p></div></div><div class="ssr-variant hidden-1liiojr hidden-rsfvs"><div class="framer-us3gxf" data-framer-name="Related Post" data-framer-component-type="RichTextContainer" style="justify-content:center;transform:none"><p dir="auto" style="--font-selector:R0Y7SW50ZXIgVGlnaHQtNjAw;--framer-font-family:&quot;Inter Tight&quot;, &quot;Inter Tight Placeholder&quot;, sans-serif;--framer-font-size:44px;--framer-font-weight:600;--framer-line-height:100%;--framer-text-color:rgb(255, 255, 255)" class="framer-text">Related <span style="--font-selector:R0Y7SW50ZXIgVGlnaHQtMzAw;--framer-font-weight:300" class="framer-text">Posts</span></p></div></div><div class="ssr-variant hidden-14od9f9 hidden-1liiojr"><div class="framer-us3gxf" data-framer-name="Related Post" data-framer-component-type="RichTextContainer" style="justify-content:center;transform:none"><p dir="auto" style="--font-selector:R0Y7SW50ZXIgVGlnaHQtNjAw;--framer-font-family:&quot;Inter Tight&quot;, &quot;Inter Tight Placeholder&quot;, sans-serif;